<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.9.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02239</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.9.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4f466432-776e-43b1-a130-5a62c5eda591</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<para>For questions 1 – 5, solve each quadratic equation by rewriting it in factored form and using the zero product property.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_39ac4015-3c0f-469f-9d92-50f25665caf2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2 + 11x + 35 = 7\)</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x = -4\) and \(x = -7\)</para><para>\(x = -6\) and \(x = -7\)</para><para>\(x = 14\) and \(x = -3\)</para><para>\(x = -5\) and \(x = -7\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.9.2.</para><para>The correct answer is \(x = -4\) and \(x = -7\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="bc01d2d3-654d-4299-a9bf-ab2cd1a59ca7" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2− 14x + 20 = -25\)</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x = 3\) and \(x = 15\)</para><para>\(x = 10\) and \(x = 2\)</para><para>\(x = -10\) and \(x = -4\)</para><para>\(x = 9\) and \(x = 5\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.9.2.</para><para>The correct answer is \(x = 9\) and \(x = 5\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="e6e8fb88-e85e-47d0-8e44-736e7880a25f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2− 6x − 52 = -12\)</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x = -16\) and \(x = 10\)</para><para>\(x = 8\) and \(x = -8\)</para><para>\(x = 10\) and \(x = -4\)</para><para>\(x = -8\) and \(x = 5\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.9.2.
      </para><para>The correct answer is \(x = 10\) and \(x = -4\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="e16128f0-fbba-4cb5-b03e-9004f3f6126d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2 − x − 64 = -8\)</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x = 8\) and \(x = -8\)</para><para>\(x = -7\) and \(x = 8\)</para><para>\(x = 28\) and \(x = -2\)</para><para>\(x = 4\) and \(x = -14\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.9.2.</para><para>The correct answer is \(x = -7\) and \(x = 8\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_1f6011d4-4680-4f06-a947-a1a6a4fb9ad4" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2 + 9x + 13 = -5\)</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x = -3\) and \(x = -6\)</para><para>\(x = -9\) and \(x = -2\)</para><para>\(x = 8\) and \(x = 1\)</para><para>\(x = 3\) and \(x = 6\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.9.2.</para><para>The correct answer is \(x = -3\) and \(x = -6\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_14506be6-f1cd-4c73-8dc7-1026c3b54b75" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Without graphing, identify the function whose graph has only one zero.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2− 9\)</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2− 13x + 42\)</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2 + 6x + 5\)</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2 − 8x + 16\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.9.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(f(x) = x^2 − 8x + 16\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_636fec47-b971-4621-aa0b-ab8977ee1b6b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Without graphing, identify the function whose graph has only one zero.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2+ 7x − 30\)</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2+ x − 30\)</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2+ 12x + 36\)</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2 − 10x + 9\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.9.3.
      </para><para>The correct answer is \(f(x) = x^2+ 12x + 36\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_65e14f56-ac7d-46fd-82e9-43eb8625935e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Without graphing, identify the function whose graph has two zeros.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2− 81\)</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2− 8x + 16\)</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2+ 8x + 16\)</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2− 12x + 36\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.9.3.
      </para><para>The correct answer is \(f(x) = x^2− 81\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="c4a59048-a9e1-4ed7-a225-a804f579731d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Without graphing, identify the function whose graph has two zeros.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2− 10x + 25\)</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2+ 4x − 21\)</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2+16x + 64\)</para><para>\(f(x) = x^2− 14x + 49\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.9.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(f(x) = x^2+ 4x − 21\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_39a9a1bd-5d3a-4f62-aeb0-f5ab200dc47c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Solve the equation: \(x^2− 18x + 90 = 9\).</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x = -9\)</para><para>\(x = -9\) and \(x=9\)</para><para>\(x= -10\) and \(x=8\)</para><para>\(x=9\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.9.4.
      </para><para>The correct answer is \(x=9\).
      </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
